Classic GRUB Legacy ordering bug. The initrd line is getting clobbered because a second kernel stanza (or a kernel line pointing to a compressed zImage/bzImage) is being parsed after it.

What happened:

kernel /vmlinuz-...        ← correct
initrd /initramfs-...      ← correct  
kernel /bzImage            ← this reset the kernel, wiped initrd pointer

GRUB Legacy resets the initrd slot when it hits a second kernel directive.

At the GRUB prompt, recover manually:

grub> root (hd0,0)
grub> kernel /vmlinuz-2.6.xx-xx.fc9.i686 root=/dev/VolGroup00/LogVol00 ro rhgb
grub> initrd /initramfs-2.6.xx-xx.fc9.i686.img
grub> boot

Single stanza, no second kernel line — boots clean.

Once booted, audit grub.conf for the rogue stanza:
bash

grep -n "^kernel\|^initrd\|^title" /boot/grub/grub.conf

Look for a stanza that has two kernel lines or a bare kernel /bzImage entry with no matching initrd. Delete the entire offending title block.

Also verify:
bash

ls -la /boot/

Check whether a raw bzImage or zImage actually exists in /boot/ — if it was staged there by something external, that's worth flagging given your threat model.

