Check for relevant skills

Here's the full LILO migration for Fedora 9:

PHASE 1 — Install LILO, remove GRUB:
bash

# Install lilo
yum install lilo

# Optional: keep grub package but disable it
# Do NOT run grub-install after this point

PHASE 2 — Gather UUIDs and memory values:
bash

# Get UUIDs for all partitions
blkid

# Example output you'll use:
# /dev/sda1: UUID="abc123..." TYPE="ext3"
# /dev/VolGroup00/LogVol00: UUID="def456..." TYPE="ext3"
# /dev/VolGroup00/LogVol01: UUID="ghi789..." TYPE="swap"

# Get total RAM for lilo's "mem=" parameter
grep MemTotal /proc/meminfo
# or
free -m

# Get exact partition geometry for lilo
fdisk -l /dev/sda

# Confirm LVM volume group and logical volumes
vgdisplay
lvdisplay

PHASE 3 — Build /etc/lilo.conf:
bash

cat > /etc/lilo.conf << 'EOF'
# /etc/lilo.conf - CTW Fedora 9 LILO configuration
# Generated: replace UUIDs from blkid output

# Global section
boot=/dev/sda
map=/boot/map
install=/boot/boot.b
prompt
timeout=50
default=Fedora9
lba32
compact

# Append kernel params — replace MEM_VALUE with MemTotal from /proc/meminfo
# rounded down to nearest 64MB boundary
append="ro rhgb quiet mem=2048M selinux=1 enforcing=1"

# Password protect interactive edit (optional but recommended)
# password=CHANGEME
# restricted

# Primary kernel stanza
image=/boot/vmlinuz-2.6.25-14.fc9.i686
    label=Fedora9
    initrd=/boot/initramfs-2.6.25-14.fc9.i686.img
    root=/dev/VolGroup00/LogVol00
    read-only

# Fallback to previous kernel if available
image=/boot/vmlinuz-2.6.25-14.fc9.i686.PAE
    label=Fedora9PAE
    initrd=/boot/initramfs-2.6.25-14.fc9.i686.PAE.img
    root=/dev/VolGroup00/LogVol00
    read-only
EOF

Verify your exact kernel filenames first:
bash

ls -la /boot/vmlinuz* /boot/initramfs*

PHASE 4 — Build /etc/fstab with UUIDs:
bash

# First collect all UUIDs
blkid -o export /dev/sda1
blkid -o export /dev/VolGroup00/LogVol00
blkid -o export /dev/VolGroup00/LogVol01

# Then write fstab — substitute actual UUIDs from blkid output
cat > /etc/fstab << 'EOF'
# /etc/fstab - CTW Fedora 9
# <device>                                <mnt>    <type>   <options>              <dump> <pass>

# Root LV
UUID=REPLACE_ROOT_UUID                    /        ext3     defaults,noatime        1      1

# Boot partition (if separate /boot on sda1)
UUID=REPLACE_BOOT_UUID                    /boot    ext3     defaults,noatime        1      2

# Swap LV
UUID=REPLACE_SWAP_UUID                    swap     swap     defaults                0      0

# Proc / sysfs / devpts (no UUID needed)
proc                                      /proc    proc     defaults                0      0
sysfs                                     /sys     sysfs    defaults                0      0
devpts                                    /dev/pts devpts   gid=5,mode=620          0      0
tmpfs                                     /dev/shm tmpfs    defaults                0      0
EOF

One-liner to auto-substitute UUIDs inline:
bash

# Extract UUIDs directly into variables
ROOT_UUID=$(blkid -s UUID -o value /dev/VolGroup00/LogVol00)
BOOT_UUID=$(blkid -s UUID -o value /dev/sda1)
SWAP_UUID=$(blkid -s UUID -o value /dev/VolGroup00/LogVol01)

echo "Root: $ROOT_UUID"
echo "Boot: $BOOT_UUID"
echo "Swap: $SWAP_UUID"

# Then sed-substitute into fstab
sed -i "s/REPLACE_ROOT_UUID/$ROOT_UUID/" /etc/fstab
sed -i "s/REPLACE_BOOT_UUID/$BOOT_UUID/" /etc/fstab
sed -i "s/REPLACE_SWAP_UUID/$SWAP_UUID/" /etc/fstab

PHASE 5 — Install LILO to MBR:
bash

# Dry run first — check for errors without writing
lilo -t -v

# If clean, write to MBR
lilo -v

# Verify map file was written
ls -la /boot/map

lilo -v output should show each label found and end with Added Fedora9 * — the asterisk marks the default.

PHASE 6 — Harden and verify:
bash

# Confirm LILO is in MBR, not GRUB
dd if=/dev/sda bs=512 count=1 | strings | head -5
# Should show "LILO" not "GRUB"

# Lock lilo.conf permissions
chmod 600 /etc/lilo.conf
chown root:root /etc/lilo.conf

# Verify fstab parses cleanly before reboot
mount -a --fake 2>&1
# or
findmnt --verify

Important: Every time you edit lilo.conf you must re-run lilo -v to rewrite the map. Unlike GRUB, LILO does not auto-detect config changes at boot — the map file is static.

