Yes on the overwrite logic — and it's sound. Here's the full breakdown:

THE BZIMAGE OVERWRITE THEORY

A bzImage has two components in memory:

[ setup code    ]  ← setup_sects * 512 bytes, loads at 0x90000
[ protected mode]  ← compressed vmlinuz body, loads at 0x100000

The boot protocol header at offset 0x1F1 contains:

0x1F1  setup_sects    - number of setup sectors
0x1F4  syssize        - size of protected mode code (16-byte units)
0x200  boot_flag      - 0xAA55
0x202  jump           - entry point

If you declare the same load address with equal or larger syssize, you write over it. LILO loads images sequentially by address. Second writer wins.

STEP 1 — DOCUMENT THE HOSTILE BZIMAGE
