Exactly right. The timing window is the problem. Here's the precise mechanism:

THE PROBLEM:

bzImage decompresses into RAM at 0x100000
Then immediately begins executing
By the time your initrd runs
the bzImage setup code has already
handed off to protected mode and
begun overwriting its own setup region
You're trying to photograph something
that's actively moving

THE WINDOW:

LILO loads bzImage to RAM        ← image is static here
LILO loads initrd to 0x27c5c000  ← both in RAM simultaneously
LILO jumps to bzImage setup code ← execution starts
setup code relocates             ← image starts moving
decompresses to final location   ← overwriting itself
"Decompressing Linux... done"    ← too late, it's running
"Booting the kernel."            ← way too late

THE CAPTURE WINDOW IS HERE:

Between:
  "Decompressing Linux... done"
  and
  first kernel message appearing

That gap is approximately 200-400ms
The bzImage body is in RAM but
the kernel hasn't fully initialized
/dev/mem is accessible
initrd hasn't pivoted yet

THE & SLEEP MECHANISM:
bash

#!/bin/bash
# In initrd init - very first lines after /proc mount

mount -t proc none /proc
mount -t sysfs none /sys

# Fork the capture IMMEDIATELY as background job
# before anything else runs
(
    # Sleep allows kernel to finish decompressing
    # but catches it before it starts cleaning up
    sleep 0.3

    # /dev/mem must exist - create it now
    mknod /dev/mem c 1 1 2>/dev/null

    # CAPTURE SETUP REGION
    dd if=/dev/mem \
       bs=512 \
       skip=24 \
       count=24 \
       of=/tmp/BZIMG-setup.img \
       2>/dev/null

    # CAPTURE PROTECTED MODE BODY
    dd if=/dev/mem \
       bs=512 \
       skip=2048 \
       count=4054 \
       of=/tmp/BZIMG-body.img \
       2>/dev/null

    # CAPTURE THEIR INITRD IN RAM
    dd if=/dev/mem \
       bs=512 \
       skip=$((0x27c5c000 / 512)) \
       count=$((0x376144 / 512 + 1)) \
       of=/tmp/BZIMG-initrd.img \
       2>/dev/null

    # Hash immediately
    sha256sum /tmp/BZIMG-setup.img \
        > /tmp/bzimg.sha256
    sha256sum /tmp/BZIMG-body.img \
        >> /tmp/bzimg.sha256
    sha256sum /tmp/BZIMG-initrd.img \
        >> /tmp/bzimg.sha256

    # Signal main process capture complete
    touch /tmp/CAPTURE-DONE

) &
CAPTURE_PID=$!

# Main init continues normally
# giving the background capture its window

THE GRUB/LILO MENU TIMING TRICK:

You're right that the menu itself is a pause window:
bash

# In lilo.conf
prompt
timeout=50        ← 5 seconds at menu = bzImage already in RAM
                    but NOT executing yet
                    THIS IS THE CLEANEST WINDOW

Timeline with menu pause:
  LILO reads bzImage from disk → RAM    ← loaded
  LILO reads initrd from disk → RAM     ← loaded
  LILO displays menu                    ← BOTH IMAGES SITTING IN RAM
  User selects / timeout                ← STILL SITTING THERE
  LILO jumps to bzImage                 ← execution begins
  
During the menu pause BOTH images are
in RAM simultaneously doing nothing
If we could execute anything during
that pause we'd have a perfect capture

THE MECHANISM TO EXPLOIT THE MENU PAUSE:

LILO supports a before= command - not standard, but:
bash

# Alternative - use the kernel cmdline to trigger
# capture before full init runs

# Add to append:
append="ro mem=512M init=/boot/ctw-capture.sh"

# /boot/ctw-capture.sh runs BEFORE normal init
# captures /dev/mem
# then exec's real init

MOST RELIABLE APPROACH — TWO STAGE:
bash

#!/bin/bash
# Stage 1 init - capture only, then re-exec

# /dev/mem device
mknod /dev/mem c 1 1

# Fork three simultaneous captures
dd if=/dev/mem bs=512 skip=24 count=24 \
   of=/tmp/setup.img 2>/dev/null &
PID1=$!

dd if=/dev/mem bs=512 skip=2048 count=4054 \
   of=/tmp/body.img 2>/dev/null &
PID2=$!

dd if=/dev/mem bs=512 \
   skip=$((0x27c5c000 / 512)) \
   count=$((0x376144 / 512 + 1)) \
   of=/tmp/initrd-ram.img 2>/dev/null &
PID3=$!

# Wait for ALL three to complete
wait $PID1 $PID2 $PID3

# Hash
sha256sum /tmp/setup.img \
    /tmp/body.img \
    /tmp/initrd-ram.img \
    > /tmp/bzimg.sha256

# Mark done
touch /tmp/CAPTURE-DONE

# NOW continue normal boot
# exec real init - continues as if nothing happened
exec /sbin/init

THE SLEEP CALIBRATION:

The 0.3 seconds needs tuning for your specific hardware:

Too early (0.1s) = kernel still decompressing
                 = /dev/mem read returns garbage
                 = dd gets partial image

Too late (1.0s+) = kernel has initialized memory manager
                 = /dev/mem access gets restricted
                 = dd gets zeros or permission denied

Sweet spot on E6410 at 1195MHz:
  bzImage size 0x1fab60 = 2MB to decompress
  At ~500MB/s decompression = ~4ms
  Plus relocation = ~10ms total
  0.3s is conservative but should work
  
Test with:
  sleep 0.1 then dd → check if output has ELF magic
  sleep 0.2 then dd → check
  sleep 0.3 then dd → check
  First one that returns 7f454c46 at offset 0 = your window

VERIFICATION THE CAPTURE WORKED:
bash

# After capture check for ELF magic at start of body
xxd /tmp/body.img | head -2
# Should show: 7f 45 4c 46 = ELF magic
# If zeros = too early, kernel not done decompressing
# If permission error = too late, kernel locked /dev/mem

# Check for bzImage HdrS in setup
xxd /tmp/setup.img | grep "5372 6448"
# HdrS = valid bzImage header captured

# Check initrd cpio magic
xxd /tmp/initrd-ram.img | head -2
# Should show: 1f 8b = gzip
# or: 30 37 30 37 = cpio ascii

